---
// The money page (WEBSITE.md §5.5), in the posture the maintainer chose in
// session (#116, 2026-08-20): INTENT PLUS DIRECT CLUB LINKS.
//
// Amended 2026-08-27, and the amendment is the whole point of the page now.
// This block used to say "the structure for money to follow the trail it
// came from is designed (features/PRICING_MODEL.md) and nothing is charged
// before it is built" - i.e. that routing a hiker's money through OurHike to
// an organization was the plan, merely unbuilt. It is not the plan. The
// maintainer settled it: "There is no funding model today for the orgs. The
// hope is we will drive membership and donations to those orgs." Money never
// passes through OurHike, by design rather than by not having got to it yet,
// and features/PRICING_MODEL.md's revenue-routing sections are superseded
// rather than deferred (pricing value #6 there).
//
// What this page must make unmistakable is the differentiator: where money
// goes - directly to them, never through us - and that nothing
// safety-relevant is ever behind it.
//
// The giving links point at the organisations' own front doors rather than
// deep links to donation forms - their sites move, ours should not claim to
// know their layout, and both put giving front and centre themselves.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'

const title = 'Support the trail — OurHike'
const description =
  'How OurHike is funded, the two commitments that hold either way, and how to give directly to the organizations that maintain these trails today.'
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <main class="wrap">
    <header class="section">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>Where the money goes</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="lede">
        OurHike takes no donations today, and it will never take one on a trail
        organization's behalf. Money you give the trail belongs with the
        organizations who maintain it, and it should reach them without passing
        through us &mdash; so it doesn't. That is a decision, not an oversight
        and not a stage we are waiting out. Two commitments hold either way:
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="section--tight">
      <div class="cards" style="max-width: 980px">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Nothing safety-relevant is ever behind a paywall.</h3>
          <p>
            Water, closures, hazards, and the map itself stay free for
            everyone, always. If paid tiers ever exist they cover convenience,
            never safety — staying safe on the trail must not require a
            subscription. That is the point of the project, not a promotion.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>The trail needs money today — give it directly.</h3>
          <p>
            The
            <a href="https://appalachiantrail.org/">Appalachian Trail
            Conservancy</a>, clubs like the
            <a href="https://www.nynjtc.org/">New York–New Jersey Trail
            Conference</a>, and preserves like
            <a href="https://www.mohonkpreserve.org/">Mohonk Preserve</a>
            maintain the ground this app describes, and take donations now, on
            their own sites — they deserve them. NY State Parks and NYS DEC
            maintain trails here too, funded through the state rather than a
            donation page. We are not affiliated with any of them — we just
            think your money belongs with the people holding the tools.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section--tight prose">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>If OurHike ever charges</h2>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 12px">
        It would be for its own convenience features and nothing else:
        optional passes for long-distance use — a thru-hike
        pass, regional passes, an all-access ceiling — and a volunteer
        exemption so the people doing trail work never pay for the map of the
        trail they maintain. <strong>What those passes would not do is send
        money to the trail organizations.</strong> They would cover OurHike's
        own costs. The organizations are supported the way this page already
        says: you give to them directly, on their own sites, and nothing you
        give them touches us.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 12px">
        None of it is live, no dates are promised, and if it ever does arrive
        it will be on this page with the accounting alongside it. Payments
        would only ever happen here on the web, never inside the app, so
        app-store fees never take a cut of what OurHike charges for itself.
        They could never have taken a cut of trail money either way &mdash;
        that money does not pass through us to be cut.
      </p>
      <p>
        Running costs today are small and covered by the volunteers building
        it: the map data is served from commodity storage, the site is static
        hosting, and the design goal is that it stays cheap enough to be
        inherited (<a
          href="https://github.com/OurHike/OurHike/blob/main/OurHikeValues.md"
          >value #7</a
        >).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section--tight prose" style="padding-bottom: 24px">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>Other ways to help</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Report what you find on the trail from inside the app — a dry spring, a
        blowdown, a washed-out crossing reaches a moderator who can act on it.
        Or help build the thing itself:
        <a href="https://github.com/OurHike/OurHike">the whole project is open
        source</a>, and the issue tracker is where the work is.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
